{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01MxjXFqfQ2rgpLuANVA7abw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Scalar single precision arithmetic that reproduces, bit for bit, what the C\n2\t//! program does when compiled for x86-64 (SSE `mulss` / `addss` / `subss`).\n3\t//!\n4\t//! For finite/infinite operands these are plain IEEE-754 operations, so the\n5\t//! ordinary Rust operators are used. The one observable difference between\n6\t//! Rust's and C's generated code is *which* NaN is propagated when an operand\n7\t//! is NaN: SSE returns the first NaN operand (destination before source), and\n8\t//! the C compiler keeps the destination as the left-hand operand of the source\n9\t//! expression. Since `printf(\"%f\")` prints the sign of a NaN (`nan` vs `-nan`),\n10\t//! that choice is visible in the program's output, so it is modelled\n11\t//! explicitly here instead of being left to the optimizer.\n12\t\n13\t/// Quiet a NaN the way the hardware does (set the quiet bit, keep sign/payload).\n14\tfn quiet(x: f32) -> f32 {\n15\t    f32::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n16\t}\n17\t\n18\t/// `a * b` with SSE NaN propagation (`a` is the destination register).\n19\tpub fn mul(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n20\t    if a.is_nan() {\n21\t        quiet(a)\n22\t    } else if b.is_nan() {\n23\t        quiet(b)\n24\t    } else {\n25\t        a * b\n26\t    }\n27\t}\n28\t\n29\t/// `a + b` with SSE NaN propagation (`a` is the destination register).\n30\tpub fn add(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n31\t    if a.is_nan() {\n32\t        quiet(a)\n33\t    } else if b.is_nan() {\n34\t        quiet(b)\n35\t    } else {\n36\t        a + b\n37\t    }\n38\t}\n39\t\n40\t/// `a - b` with SSE NaN propagation (`a` is the destination register).\n41\tpub fn sub(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n42\t    if a.is_nan() {\n43\t        quiet(a)\n44\t    } else if b.is_nan() {\n45\t        quiet(b)\n46\t    } else {\n47\t        a - b\n48\t    }\n49\t}\n50\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5d27ebf1-e727-4f79-ae2e-169df46ae7cb","uuid":"17744a5c-8a69-4dae-9d4b-b79f1f698d61","timestamp":"2026-08-27T15:27:53.433Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-QM7tPW/translation/src/fpu.rs","content":"//! Scalar single precision arithmetic that reproduces, bit for bit, what the C\n//! program does when compiled for x86-64 (SSE `mulss` / `addss` / `subss`).\n//!\n//! For finite/infinite operands these are plain IEEE-754 operations, so the\n//! ordinary Rust operators are used. The one observable difference between\n//! Rust's and C's generated code is *which* NaN is propagated when an operand\n//! is NaN: SSE returns the first NaN operand (destination before source), and\n//! the C compiler keeps the destination as the left-hand operand of the source\n//! expression. Since `printf(\"%f\")` prints the sign of a NaN (`nan` vs `-nan`),\n//! that choice is visible in the program's output, so it is modelled\n//! explicitly here instead of being left to the optimizer.\n\n/// Quiet a NaN the way the hardware does (set the quiet bit, keep sign/payload).\nfn quiet(x: f32) -> f32 {\n    f32::from_bits(x.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n}\n\n/// `a * b` with SSE NaN propagation (`a` is the destination register).\npub fn mul(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n    if a.is_nan() {\n        quiet(a)\n    } else if b.is_nan() {\n        quiet(b)\n    } else {\n        a * b\n    }\n}\n\n/// `a + b` with SSE NaN propagation (`a` is the destination register).\npub fn add(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n    if a.is_nan() {\n        quiet(a)\n    } else if b.is_nan() {\n        quiet(b)\n    } else {\n        a + b\n    }\n}\n\n/// `a - b` with SSE NaN propagation (`a` is the destination register).\npub fn sub(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {\n    if a.is_nan() {\n        quiet(a)\n    } else if b.is_nan() {\n        quiet(b)\n    } else {\n        a - b\n    }\n}\n","numLines":50,"startLine":1,"totalLines":50}}}
